+# Closed attempt: memory accounting diagnosis
+
+The fixed f9b/a8b attempt remains FAILED, with no retry. The actual outer receipt records406,568,960 aggregate sampled bytes against402,653,184: excess3,915,776 bytes (about3.735MiB), at1.845080 seconds of outer timer. Production directory exists but is empty. No checkpoint/partial or numerical certificate survived. No physical action, norm scan, benchmark, or launch was performed for this diagnosis.
+
+## What the evidence establishes
+
+The cap was on the sum of outer process, bootstrap/supervisor and worker descendants, including time wrappers. The earlier real action cost reported roughly199.5MB for a single worker (tree roughly222.3MB under its simpler wrapper). That is not the aggregate peak of this integrated hierarchy. The forecast priced runtime but did not construct an enforceable live-memory ledger for the whole simultaneous process tree. This is the concrete missing forecast component.
+
+The integrated candidate also holds more arrays than the isolated action fixture. Every full real vector is8MiB. At initial solve, vacuum/source, x, r and p already occupy up to32MiB before the action output/workspace (vacuum initially uses zero pages, so committed RSS is not identical to allocation). Later iterations keep ap from the previous iteration until the new action returns, adding another8MiB. The isolated action fixture primarily supplied one input vector. During action, output, neighbor tmp, center term, replacement multiply/add outputs coexist at statement boundaries. Real kernel operations are out-of-place. Later stages would additionally retain first representatives, second representatives and transported/source arrays; therefore a successful first checkpoint would not itself certify the worst memory stage.
+
+Source pin checking also has a transient/allocator-retention cost in each process. Outer/bootstrap and worker sha functions use Path.read_bytes. The largest pinned objects are each16,119,328 bytes (Python framework and libpython path); the total pinned111,962,237 bytes is NOT simultaneously read and must not be counted as a111MB allocation. A single whole-file hashing buffer is about15.37MiB, and freed allocator pages may remain resident. Supervisor sha streams1MiB, but the outer/bootstrap/worker whole-file readers do not. Runtime JSON itself is only405KB; coefficient/ADAPTED/BLOCKS files total under100KB, so those source payload sizes alone do not explain the peak. Expanded Python objects and imported NumPy/stdlib/process baselines contribute separately.
+
+The receipt has no per-PID RSS time series or worker stage marker before the first certificate. Consequently we cannot attribute the measured406.6MB exactly between baseline, hashing and candidate arrays, or prove which arithmetic statement was executing. It is plausible that combined wrapper baseline plus integrated CG work arrays exhausted the missing aggregate allowance. Claiming a measured worker-only peak or a specific first-action failure would exceed the saved evidence. The root watchdog correctly closed the attempt.
+
+## A genuinely different future memory architecture
+
+If pursued in a separately frozen campaign, use one minimal stdlib supervisor plus one worker, eliminating the resident bootstrap layer by exec-replacing it with the verified worker process (or executing the supervisor in the outer process). Preserve independent outer enforcement, but make the actual number of resident interpreters part of the design. Stream every hash in bounded chunks. Create a memory ledger for maximum simultaneously live real buffers and explicitly release/reload representatives from verified disk files before the second-source and final-assembly phases.
+
+A worker redesign should own a fixed workspace rather than rely on allocation/free behavior: preallocated CG x/r/p/ap, reused action scratch buffers, in-place updates only where arithmetic order is retained or recertified, and streaming output/hash. Each altered arithmetic path requires same-kernel tiny CAR and fresh residual controls; reusing the old time benchmark is insufficient. Avoid adding21 full permutation tables. Preserve exact norms and all certificate obligations.
+
+The cheapest informative NEW cost contract would use the complete final supervisor/worker topology, source checks, and a synthetic allocation schedule that commits/touches the worst declared buffers, plus one fixed non-solving action if separately authorized. It must cover retained-representative/source phases and checkpoint I/O, report per-PID/time/stage RSS and exact buffer ledger, and include all loader baselines. A conservative prospective acceptance target could be<=320MiB whole tree under the unchanged384MiB hard cap. This is a design suggestion, not an authorized profile or evidence it fits. Do not merely rerun the failed attempt or relax its cap.
