+# Cold review: conditional nonlinear-vertex implications
+
+Disposition: PASS for the declared conditional statements. Complete source read: native-l6-nonlinear-vertex-implications/DERIVATION.md, SHA256 c2c2a4b06b603e7032e27d91d407bc5e2e2a783fb1bd30512b613fb097ca2dae. No physical vector, eigensolver, or resolvent action was run. Existing independently reviewed adapted-frame and arithmetic-error proofs are reused as inputs, not relabeled as fresh independent derivations; this reviewer authored portions of those inputs.
+
+The all108-mode variational identity is correct. Relative to the fixed canonical vacuum, all complex linear CAR insertions have exactly the full one-particle space as their vacuum image. Real coefficients multiplying the two Hermitian Majoranas of each mode also span an arbitrary complex creation coefficient on the vacuum. The odd transition's orthogonal residual is therefore exactly its higher-particle part. Outside-W creation amplitudes cannot improve the approximation, since chi has no outside-W occupation. The operator-norm lower bound follows by evaluating on one normalized vacuum and is explicitly conditional on a full operator being defined. It does not identify full operator norm or excited-state action.
+
+The normalized-state formula also checks: maximizing overlap of chi/||chi|| with a normalized one-particle vector gives sqrt(1-w/T), so squared distance is2-2sqrt(1-w/T). This differs from the unrestricted-amplitude projection problem. For w=T the optimum overlap is zero and the squared distance is2. The statement appropriately requires T>0.
+
+The stored-vector correction uses only contraction of the high-particle projector and the reverse triangle inequality. A full error E and complete high-sector stored norm imply the declared distance lower bound. Positive floating weight alone is insufficient; an interval touching zero gives no obstruction. The basis qualification is accurate: simultaneous Gaussian transport of operator/state/projectors is harmless, whereas changing the vacuum or using nonlinear dressing changes the question. Nothing here excludes all dressed quadratic descriptions.
+
+Frequency normalization independently recomputed: six sqrt12 modes contribute12sqrt3, six sqrt24 contribute12sqrt6, six sqrt36 contribute36, and three sqrt48 contribute12sqrt3. Their sum is exactly LambdaW=36+24sqrt3+12sqrt6. The minimum possible higher odd excitation is three distinct lowest modes, energy3sqrt12=6sqrt3; six such modes are available. The maximum is the fully occupied21-mode state, which has odd parity and energyLambdaW. Thus the stated reciprocal bounds on the positive high-sector resolvent are valid and attain the spectral extremes on the declared W Fock space. There is no factor of two missing relative to the already reviewed excitation-frequency convention. Restoring |t| scales both energy bounds; the weight must refer to the correspondingly normalized physical transition, whose resolvent-generated amplitude itself depends on |t|.
+
+The proposed binning error is correct: for A=P_high Hact^-1 P_high, ||A||<=1/(6sqrt3), and x=P_high chihat, delta=P_high(chi-chihat), the quadratic-form difference is bounded by ||A||(2||x||E+E²). Number conservation makes frequency-occupation bins diagonal, so exact dyadic squares and rational radical enclosures suffice without a new inverse solve. This proposed scan is not currently executed or certified as cheap.
+
+The source correctly confines Qhigh to a positive singleton susceptibility. It does not equate it with an off-site closure, the full singleton contribution to a specified spectator coefficient, or mixed-middle histories. The global-i storage phase drops out of all norms and this diagonal quadratic form. The contrasting L4 vacuum-linear but operator-nonlinear example is used in the correct logical direction. No missing premise or correction found.
